  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1000 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled conditions return tonight through \nSaturday with daily chances for showers and thunderstorms.\n\nLatest wx analysis shows an upper level trough over the Great Lakes \nregion with a ridge over western Atlantic, causing SW flow aloft and \na surface stationary boundary draped near/ just N of the VA/NC \nstate border. Slow-moving scattered showers and storms developed\nmainly just to our northwest along a pre-frontal trough. Given\nvery weak steering flow, these storms have produced some locally\nheavy rainfall just to our W-NW of the US-15 corridor. Latest\nmesoanalysis showing a weak covergence boundary developing just\nNW of RIC at this hour, and the past few runs of CAMs have shown\nsome scattered storms pushing across our NW tier of counties\nthrough the early overnight hours (approximately Cumberland to\nAshland to Tappahannock), and have nudged PoPs into the 40-60% \nrange in this area, highest western sections. Minimal if any \nsevere threat left over, but frequent lightning and heavy \nrainfall are possible. Farther south, some very low PoPs have \nbeen maintained across far SE counties along the Albemarle \nSound.\n\nThe boundary drops SE tonight and settles into interior SE VA \nand the Carolina Coastal Plain by Friday, but remains rather \ndiffuse. There is a strengthening jet core over New England, but\nthere remains some uncertainty as to how well the RRQ of this \nfeature interacts with the surface boundary. The 12z HREF \ncontinues to a decent signal for locally heavy rain from \nshowers/tstms with a contour of a 30% chc of 3\"/3hr across \neastern/SE VA and NE NC to the Richmond/Tri-City metro areas. \nWPC has expanded the Slight Risk of Excessive Rainfall slightly \nnorth, now covering the southern half of the FA. Locally heavy \nrainfall is the main threat tomorrow, but a few stronger storms \nare possible with damaging winds possible, with 20-25 kt of SW \nflow aloft. SPC maintains a Marginal Risk of severe storms for \nthe entire area Friday. Showers/storms look to be scattered to \nwidespread based on the latest guidance. PoPs are now 50-70% for\na large portion of the area after 2 PM into the evening. \nLocally heavy rainfall will be possible in any showers/tstms. \nDrier conditions arrive by Sunday with only low PoPs over \ninterior SE VA/interior NE NC.\n\nSeasonally hot and humid today through Saturday with high \ntemperatures primarily in the mid to upper 80s, and nudging up to \nthe upper 80s/around 90F (mid 80s at the coast) Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to \nmiddle portion of next week.\n\nHot conditions return early-mid next week as a 1.5-2.0 st dev 500mb \nridge builds from the Central Plains to the Southeast. NBM high \ntemperatures Monday through Wednesday are primarily in the lower to \nmid 90s. However, 850mb temperatures are primarily 19-21C in the 12 \nz/27 EPS/GEFS, with similar values in the deterministic 00z ECMWF. \nThis along with 1000-850mb thickness schemes support highs in the \nmid to upper 90s. The 12z GFS continues to be even hotter, but \nremains an outlier. If the hotter temperatures are realized that \nwill mean deeper mixing, which should allow dewpoints to drop into \nthe upper 60s/around 70F during peak heating. Regardless, this would \nsupport heat indices into the lower 100s. A developing negative \nheight anomaly near 40N/50W may cause the eastern portion of the \nridge break down/retrograde later next week. However, Thursday \nlikely remains quite hot as well. Mainly dry with PoPs near to below \nclimo Monday through Wednesday. Chances of showers/tstms may not \nreturn until Thursday once NW mid-level flow increases as the \neastern flank of the ridge breaks down.",
